# MO-1306 Phase 3B-R2: independent XML advisory review

Disposition: **REACHABLE_NOT_ATTACKER_CONTROLLED** for GHSA-8gc5-j5rx-235r. The vulnerable parser is conditionally reached in the frozen upload invocation. A repository contributor cannot choose the XML response under the frozen hosted-service trust model. This finding requires neither discretionary risk acceptance nor an Action-pin correction within this scope. It does not declare the dependency fixed.

The pinned upload-artifact Action contains fast-xml-parser **5.4.1**, through @actions/artifact 6.2.0, @azure/storage-blob 12.31.0 and @azure/core-xml 1.5.0. One dependency appears in package-lock; each of the upload and unused merge bundles contains one parser implementation. The affected parser, option builder and XMLParser modules are identical between those bundles and match upstream 5.4.1 after documented ncc import/export and symbol transformations. Exact hashes/diffs and lock SRI verification are retained in `xml-source-review.json` and `xml-sources/`.

The [maintainer advisory](https://github.com/NaturalIntelligence/fast-xml-parser/security/advisories/GHSA-8gc5-j5rx-235r) identifies affected versions through 5.5.5 and fixes 4.5.5/5.5.6. GitHub's normalized ranges are >=4.0.0-beta.3,<4.5.5 and >=5.0.0,<5.5.6. The [fixing commit](https://github.com/NaturalIntelligence/fast-xml-parser/commit/bd26122c838e6a55e7d7ac49b4ccc01a49999a01) adds expansion counting to standard and HTML entity loops. The advisory concerns availability when an attacker can supply sufficiently large XML. This review uses small local inputs only.

The actual call chain is `run → artifact.uploadArtifact → CreateArtifact.signedUploadUrl → uploadToBlobStorage → BlobClient/BlockBlobClient.uploadStream → stageBlock/commitBlockList → Azure deserializationPolicy → parse(response.bodyAsText) → core-xml.parseXML → XMLParser.parse → OrderedObjParser.replaceEntitiesValue`. XML parsing is conditional on a nonempty, nonstreamed response with XML Content-Type; a normal Azure error response reaches it without any compromise. It is not a dead import. Ordinary successful uploads have empty response bodies and do not call the parser.

Azure uses `processEntities:true`, normalized to `maxTotalExpansions:1000`, `maxEntitySize:10000`, `maxExpansionDepth:10`, and `maxExpandedLength:100000`. It leaves `htmlEntities:false`, uses text key `#`, attribute group `$`, preserves attributes and text whitespace, and disables automatic value coercion. Complete scalar, function and undefined options appear in `advisory-xml.json`.

| Local input repeated n times | 999 | 1000 | 1001 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `&#60;`, `&#x3C;`, or `&lt;` | decoded | decoded | decoded, limit bypassed |
| DOCTYPE-defined `&a;` | decoded | decoded | rejected at expansion limit |
| Generic `&#65;` with frozen options | literal | literal | literal, no decoded expansion |
| Fixed 5.5.6 control, `&#60;` | decoded | decoded | rejected |

All **33 scenarios passed their expected assertions**: 25 boundary cases (five forms at 998–1002), five fixed-version controls and three real Action-entry scenarios with offline transport substitutes. The `&#60;` 1001 case used 5,012 input bytes, returned 1,001 `<` characters, took **0.916 ms**, and increased sampled JS heap use by **500,936 bytes** (13,122,464 to 13,623,400). Sampled RSS changed from 68,448,256 to 68,595,712 bytes. This is bounded contract evidence, not a resource-exhaustion demonstration. All boundary parse times were 0.450–3.632 ms. Child processes had a 10-second wall deadline, 96 MiB V8 old-space limit, and a hard 384 MiB Windows Job process-memory cap assigned before the probe gate; peak process committed memory across scenarios was 96,665,600 bytes.

The real-entry success witness produced a zip Put Block and generated XML Put Block List with **zero parser calls**. A 400 XML error injected separately at staging and commit reached the unchanged parser once each, decoded 1001 entities and yielded `RestError`, with a 1001-character message. Distinct artifact-content sentinels were absent from every parsed XML response. GitHub RPC/backend identifiers and the final HTTP transport were substituted; zip creation, Action entry, Azure pipeline and parser were original. Network APIs were denied. These are local witnesses, not a hosted campaign.

The parser does not parse artifact or repository bytes as XML. Under the [Put Block contract](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/storageservices/put-block), the artifact zip is an opaque block body. [Put Block List](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/storageservices/put-block-list) contains generated base64 UUID/counter IDs, never artifact contents. The fixed artifact name uses GitHub run ID/attempt. URLs/SAS are selected by GitHub; request metadata is fixed or generated, and content lengths are decimal byte counts. Service errors may reflect operation fields, but those fields offer no arbitrary repository-controlled entity syntax. The documented contract does not reflect opaque blob bytes into XML errors. Changing artifact size can influence the response selected without controlling its XML syntax.

The entity limit partially protects DOCTYPE processing. The maintainer's `htmlEntities:false` workaround disables generic numeric/HTML decoding but does not protect the standard/numeric-alias path in exact 5.4.1. XML syntax validation also does not enforce that limit. Neither is claimed as a complete mitigation.

Malicious XML can still arrive from a compromised/misbehaving Azure endpoint, a GitHub artifact service returning a hostile signed URL, or failure of trusted runner/proxy/CA/TLS infrastructure. The SDK sets `allowInsecureConnection:true`; it does not impose a hostname allowlist. Default redirects are same-origin, rejecting cross-origin or HTTPS-to-HTTP changes; normal HTTPS response substitution still requires authenticated transport failure. Thus reaching the parser does **not** require compromise, while selecting malicious XML requires one of these trusted-infrastructure failures rather than repository or artifact control. This is not a claim that only Azure compromise suffices.

The current read-only 3A-R pass/fail/CNE certification workflows have exactly the same relevant upload block and pin, and use C3AB as the trusted tool ref. Their manual dispatch, read-only contents permission, separate tool/data checkouts, fixed six output basenames and absence of PR triggers/secret inputs preserve the analyzed boundary. Future endpoint/env exposure, alternate Action invocation or pin changes require reassessment.

The prior count-bypass observation is independently reproduced, with a correction for generic numeric references remaining literal. The prior reviews' dispositions remain unchanged in their worktrees. The different final disposition follows the newly established source, runtime call path and data-origin boundary. Canonical machine-readable analysis: `evidence/mo1306/phase3b-r2/advisory-xml.json`; all supporting source bytes, hashes, timing and memory records are linked there.
